// Practice quiz: Method practice
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, so comparisons are written in words.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/methods-and-recursion/method-practice

::o1 decomposing::A program must find the average of some temperatures and report whether that average is above 30. Which design gives each part clear inputs and outputs? {
=average(int[] temps) returns a double, and isHot(double avg) returns a boolean#Correct. Each method has its inputs as parameters and one result as its return value, so each can be tested alone.
~Put every step in main, because the program is small#Not quite. It may work, but no single part can be called and checked on its own.
~average(int[] temps) prints the average and returns nothing, and isHot reads what was printed#Not quite. A method cannot read printed text; the average should be returned.
~Write one method for each line of the program#Not quite. A method should do one meaningful job, not mirror single lines.
}

::o1 easy to test::Which method is easiest to test by calling it and checking the result? {
=static int discounted(int price, int percent), which returns the reduced price#Correct. Its inputs are parameters and its output is a return value you can compare with what you expected.
~static void discounted(), which reads two shared fields and prints the reduced price#Not quite. Hidden inputs and printed output make each test harder to set up and check.
~static void discounted(int price, int percent), which prints the reduced price#Not quite. A printed result cannot be compared in code the way a returned value can.
~static int discounted(), which always returns 0#Not quite. It has no inputs, so there is nothing meaningful to test.
}

::o2 boundary test::A method isPassing(int score) accepts scores from 0 to 100 and passes a score of 60 or more. Which input is a boundary test? {
=60#Correct. 60 sits exactly at the edge where the result changes from false to true.
~75#Not quite. 75 is an ordinary input in the middle of the passing range.
~Minus 3#Not quite. That is an invalid input, outside 0 to 100.
~1000#Not quite. That is an invalid input, far outside the valid range.
}

::o2 why boundaries::Why should tests include boundary inputs? {
=Off-by-one bugs, such as greater than written where at least was meant, show up only at the edge#Correct. An ordinary input often passes even when the comparison at the edge is wrong.
~Boundary inputs are the ones users type most often#Not quite. They are chosen because behavior changes there, not because they are common.
~Boundary tests replace the need for invalid-input tests#Not quite. Invalid inputs check a different promise: that the method rejects them.
~Only boundary inputs compile#Not quite. Any int value compiles; the kinds of test differ in what they reveal.
}

::o3 before refactoring::Before you refactor duplicated code, what should you record? {
=The results the current code gives for known inputs, so you can compare them afterwards#Correct. A refactor must keep behavior, and the earlier results are what you compare against.
~How many lines the code has#Not quite. Line count says nothing about whether behavior is preserved.
~Nothing; a refactor cannot change behavior#Not quite. A careless refactor can change results, which is why you test before and after.
~Only the variable names#Not quite. Names may change in a refactor; results must not.
}

::o3 keeping behavior::Two blocks each build a name in the form Last, First from their own two String variables. Which refactor keeps the behavior? {
=A method formatName(String first, String last) that returns last + ", " + first, called by both blocks with their own names#Correct. The parts that differ become parameters, and both blocks still produce the same text as before.
~A method with no parameters that returns the first block's names#Not quite. The second block would then show the wrong name.
~A method formatName(String first, String last) that returns first + ", " + last#Not quite. Swapping the order changes the output, so behavior is not preserved.
~Keep both blocks and add a comment saying they are the same#Not quite. The duplication is still there, so this is not a refactor.
}
